      48.01,
      332.13,
      420.07,
      449.99
    ],
    "reading_order": 14,
    "text": "The given distribution being of the less than type, 140, 145, 150, . . ., 165 give the\nupper limits of the corresponding class intervals. So, the classes should be below 140,\n140 - 145, 145 - 150, . . ., 160 - 165. Observe that from the given distribution, we find\nthat there are 4 girls with height less than 140, i.e., the frequency of class interval\nbelow 140 is 4. Now, there are 11 girls with heights less than 145 and 4 girls with\nheight less than 140. Therefore, the number of girls with height in the interval\n140 - 145 is 11 – 4 = 7. Similarly, the frequency of 145 - 150 is 29 – 11 = 18, for\n150 - 155, it is 40 – 29 = 11, and so on. So, our frequency distribution  table with the\ngiven cumulative frequencies becomes:",
    "latex": null,
